<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-intro-eigenvectors" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Introduction to Eigenvectors</title>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       INTRO + REASSURANCE
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    In the last section, we saw that two linearly independent solutions of a system can be
    combined into the general solution. But now our goal is to actually find solutions in the first place.
  </p>

  <p>
    To do this, we need to understand some basics about eigenvalues and
    eigenvectors. This page is pure linear algebra, but we will apply it to differential equations in the next section.
    As before, we are only skimming a topic that one would spend a few weeks on
    in a linear algebra course. We'll build just the working understanding we
    need!
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       DEFINITION: EIGENVALUES AND EIGENVECTORS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <definition xml:id="def-eigenvalue-eigenvector">
    <title>Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        We say that <m>\lambda</m> is an <term>eigenvalue</term> of a matrix <m>A</m> if
        there is a <em>non-zero</em> vector <m>\mathbf{v}</m>, called an associated
        <term>eigenvector</term> for <m>\lambda</m>, where:
        <me>A\mathbf{v} = \lambda \mathbf{v}.</me>
        This is equivalent to:
        <me>(A - \lambda I)\mathbf{v} = \mathbf{0},</me>
        where <m>I = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}</m> is the identity
        matrix.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <p>
    That is, multiplying <m>\mathbf{v}</m> (the eigenvector) by the matrix <m>A</m> has the same effect as
    multiplying <m>\mathbf{v}</m> by the single number <m>\lambda</m> (the eigenvalue).
  </p>

  <p>
    One important observation is that if <m>\mathbf{v}</m> is an eigenvector,
    then so is any non-zero scalar multiple <m>c\mathbf{v}</m>.</p>
    <proof> <title> Justification on why non-zero multiples of eigenvectors are still eigenvectors </title>
    <p> The check is quick:
    <me>A(c\mathbf{v}) = c(A\mathbf{v}) = c(\lambda \mathbf{v}) = \lambda (c\mathbf{v}).</me>
    So <m>c\mathbf{v}</m> satisfies the same equation with the same eigenvalue
    <m>\lambda</m>. Eigenvectors are never unique.
  </p>
  </proof>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       EXPLORATION: CONFIRMING AN EIGENVALUE
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    Okay, let's try to make sure we understand this definition a bit better! </p>

  <exploration xml:id="exploration-confirm-eigenvalue">
    <title>Confirming an Eigenvalue</title>
<introduction>

<p> Suppose someone claims that <m>\lambda = -2</m>
    is an eigenvalue of
    <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 4 \amp 2 \\ 3 \amp -1 \end{bmatrix}</m>
    with eigenvector
    <m>\mathbf{v} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -3 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
    How would we confirm that? Recall that the definition says to check whether
    <m>A\mathbf{v} = \lambda \mathbf{v}</m> for some number <m>\lambda</m>.
  </p> </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-compute-av">
      <title>Compute <m>A\mathbf{v}</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Using the matrix-vector product from earlier in the chapter, compute:
          <me>A\mathbf{v} = \begin{bmatrix} 4 \amp 2 \\ 3 \amp -1 \end{bmatrix}
            \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -3 \end{bmatrix}.</me>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>A\mathbf{v} = \begin{bmatrix} -2 \\ 6 \end{bmatrix}</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct! The first component is <m>4 \cdot 1 + 2 \cdot (-3) = 4 - 6 = -2</m>,
              and the second is <m>3 \cdot 1 + (-1)(-3) = 3 + 3 = 6</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>A\mathbf{v} = \begin{bmatrix} 10 \\ 6 \end{bmatrix}</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Watch the signs! The second component of <m>\mathbf{v}</m> is <m>-3</m>, not
              <m>3</m>. The first component of <m>A\mathbf{v}</m> should be
              <m>4 \cdot 1 + 2 \cdot (-3) = 4 - 6 = -2</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>A\mathbf{v} = \begin{bmatrix} -2 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The first component is right, but check the second: it uses the
              <em>bottom row</em> of <m>A</m>, giving
              <m>3 \cdot 1 + (-1)(-3) = 3 + 3 = 6</m>. The two negatives multiply to a
              positive.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="task-interpret-av">
      <title>Compare <m>A\mathbf{v}</m> to <m>\mathbf{v}</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          From the previous task,
          <m>A\mathbf{v} = \begin{bmatrix} -2 \\ 6 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
          Now check whether it is a scalar multiple of the vector
          <m>\mathbf{v}</m> we started with. </p>
      </statement>
      <solution> <title> Verifying whether v is an eigenvector</title>
        <p>
          Yes! It is a scalar multiple of the vector we started with:
          <me>A\mathbf{v} = \begin{bmatrix} -2 \\ 6 \end{bmatrix}
            = \underbrace{-2}_{\lambda}\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -3 \end{bmatrix} = -2\mathbf{v}.</me>
          This is exactly the eigenvalue equation <m>A\mathbf{v} = \lambda\mathbf{v}</m>
          with <m>\lambda = -2</m>. Notice that both components scaled by the same
          factor: <m>1 \mapsto -2</m> and <m>-3 \mapsto 6</m> are each "multiply by
          <m>-2</m>."
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <conclusion>
      <p>
        This computation confirms that <m>\mathbf{v} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -3 \end{bmatrix}</m> is an eigenvector of <m>A</m>, with
        an eigenvalue of <m>\lambda = -2</m>.
      </p>
    </conclusion>
  </exploration>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       ACTIVITY: IS IT AN EIGENVECTOR? (MC)
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    The idea in the exploration was that <m>A\mathbf{v}</m> came out as a multiple of
    <m>\mathbf{v}</m>, with both components scaling by the <em>same</em> number. Let's do a quick
    check that you understand this in the following question.
  </p>

  <activity xml:id="act-eigenvector-or-not">
    <title>Is There an Eigenvalue?</title>
    <statement>
      <p> Suppose that for some matrix <m>A</m> and the vector <m>\mathbf{v} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}</m>, we
    found
    <m>A\mathbf{v} = \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ 5 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
      </p>

    <p>
        Is there an eigenvalue <m>\lambda</m> for which
        <m>\mathbf{v} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}</m> is an associated
        eigenvector? That is, does <m>A\mathbf{v} = \lambda\mathbf{v}</m> hold for some
        <m>\lambda</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            No, there is no such <m>\lambda</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct! For <m>\mathbf{v}</m> to be an eigenvector, <m>A\mathbf{v}</m> must be
            a multiple of <m>\mathbf{v}</m>, meaning both components scale by the same
            factor. The first component suggests <m>\lambda = 3</m>, but
            <m>3\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ 6 \end{bmatrix}
            \neq \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ 5 \end{bmatrix}</m>. No single number works for both
            components, so <m>\mathbf{v}</m> is not an eigenvector of this matrix.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Yes, with <m>\lambda = 3</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Careful: <m>\lambda = 3</m> matches the <em>first</em> component only. Checking
            the whole vector,
            <m>3\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ 6 \end{bmatrix}</m>,
            but we were told <m>A\mathbf{v} = \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ 5 \end{bmatrix}</m>. The
            second components don't agree, so <m>A\mathbf{v} \neq 3\mathbf{v}</m>. The
            eigenvalue equation must hold in <em>every</em> component at once.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Yes, with <m>\lambda = \frac{5}{2}</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Careful: <m>\lambda = \frac{5}{2}</m> matches the <em>second</em> component
            only. Checking the whole vector,
            <m>\frac{5}{2}\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}
              = \begin{bmatrix} 5/2 \\ 5 \end{bmatrix}</m>,
            which doesn't match
            <m>A\mathbf{v} = \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ 5 \end{bmatrix}</m> in the first
            component. The eigenvalue equation must hold in <em>every</em> component at
            once, with one single <m>\lambda</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </activity>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       KEY TAKEAWAYS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <conclusion>
    <title>Key Takeaways</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>
          <alert>The definition:</alert> <m>\lambda</m> is an eigenvalue of <m>A</m> with
          associated (non-zero) eigenvector <m>\mathbf{v}</m> when
          <m>A\mathbf{v} = \lambda\mathbf{v}</m>
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Multiples of both components:</alert> for <m>\mathbf{v}</m> to be an
          eigenvector, <m>A\mathbf{v}</m> must scale every component of
          <m>\mathbf{v}</m> by the same single number <m>\lambda</m>.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Eigenvectors aren't unique:</alert> any non-zero scalar multiple
          <m>c\mathbf{v}</m> of an eigenvector is also an eigenvector, with the same
          eigenvalue.
        </p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </conclusion>

